node_modules/
# Agent worktrees live here. A worktree is a second checkout of this same repo —
# committing it would put a whole duplicate copy of the project inside itself.
#
# Narrowed to the worktrees rather than all of .claude/ on purpose: agent
# definitions, skills and settings belong under .claude/ too, and those ARE worth
# tracking. Ignoring the parent would mean a future .claude/agents/*.md could
# never be committed and nobody would be told why.
.claude/worktrees/
data/commons.db
data/commons.db-journal
data/commons.db-wal
data/commons.db-shm
data/upstream/cache/
# When a card was last produced is a fact about THIS computer, not about the
# commons. It must never travel to another chapter in a clean copy. Named as a
# sidecar of the database file, like -wal and -shm, so it follows whichever
# commons is open — the old fixed path let the test suite and the prover write
# the real one.
data/*-last-card.json
data/last-card.json
app/dist/
app/node_modules/
.DS_Store
.env
.env.local

# The ecoregion library.
# The INDEX ships with the repo — it is 314 KB and nothing works without it.
# The DOSSIERS do not: ~100 KB each, ~97 MB for full US coverage, and every byte
# is regenerable with `npm run data`. Putting them in git history would make the
# repository permanently heavy for everyone who only wants their own bioregion.
# To carry them offline, build a portable bundle: npm run data -- --bundle
data/dossiers/
!data/regions/
node_modules

# Backups of the commons. Same reasoning as commons.db itself: this is the
# community's data, not the software.
data/backups/
# The backup now carries the media beside it, so the folder it writes has to be
# ignored by the same rule and for the same reason.
data/*-media/

# The evidence store: every photograph this commons holds.
#
# The most important line in this file, and the one most easily left out —
# `data/commons.db` was ignored from the beginning and this folder did not exist
# yet. Everything else here is either regenerable or merely private. This is
# neither: it is photographs, some of them of people, taken under a consent
# record that says who agreed to what and that they may withdraw it.
#
# Git history does not honour a withdrawal. A photograph committed once is in
# every clone of this repository forever, and `withdraw_media` — which deletes
# the bytes on the machine that holds them — cannot reach it. On a PUBLIC
# repository, one `git add -A` by somebody who has just started using the OS
# would publish their neighbours' faces and be unfixable.
data/media/
